Performance
===========

- Resolved: descriptor spec was re-validated on every module call — validate_descriptor →
  validate_descriptor_spec (src/main.rs) deep-copied each spec field (spec struct, then
  documentation/arity/type/return, including full copies of the type and return arrays) per
  check per call. validate_descriptor_spec now borrows the cells in place (field_cell /
  spec_borrow, no copy()); every check and error message is unchanged, and the spec is still
  read live at each call, so `set` into a bound module's descriptor takes effect immediately
  (locked by a test in src/tests/modules.rs). Benchmark (release, 100k calls to str.upper in a
  loop, best of 5): 250 ms → 178 ms — the module-call tax dropped ~40% (~0.7 µs/call), with
  plain-fn and bare-loop baselines unchanged. Residual: an (arity, type) identity cache would
  remove the remaining scans, but it would go stale if `set` mutates a spec — that belongs
  with the persistent-representation work below. (was 10)

- Resolved: deep copy on every read — the core cost of the "value semantics with explicit ^
  aliasing" design. Every variable read, field access, and index did a full recursive copy() of
  arrays/structs, and every set/bind ran a full O(n) graph walk with fresh HashSets for cycle
  detection. Model P replaces this: array/struct snapshots are immutable (Rc<Vec<Value>> /
  Rc<Vec<(String, Value)>>), so reads are O(1)-shallow (shared) clones; a ^ alias is a logical
  location (root Cell + path, resolved against the current value at read/write time — it never
  pins an intermediate node, so a whole-value rebind invalidates element/field aliases and they
  fail deterministically instead of exposing stale data); writes resolve the terminal location
  (follow cell chains + translate intermediate value aliases: write-through), rebuild the
  immutable snapshot chain along the path (whole-array/struct copy per level, O(children)), and
  replace the value in the terminal cell — aliases stay live while rebinds stay isolated. Cycle
  detection walks only when the written value contains a Ref (all 8 locked cases still say
  "cyclic reference"; sibling/sibling and helper/root chains now caught via the landing-walk).
  Benchmark (release, 100k iters, best-of-3, ns/iter; before → after A1–A3 → Model P): bare
  646→565→627, fn_int 974→891→1002, fn_arr5 1431→1350→1129, fn_arr100 7385→7295→2411 (3.0×),
  fn_nested5x5 3662→3566→1712 (2.1×), index_read 1154→992→949, field_read 1044→965→1036,
  nested_read 1148→917→1103, set_elem_scalar 928→664→906, set_nested 1044→671→1040,
  set_elem_big 10607→4521→2521 (4.2× vs before). The deep-copy tax on array-heavy work is gone
  (≈3× on fn_arr100, ≈2× on fn_nested5x5); small-scalar cases jitter ±10–60 ns from allocator
  noise. Residual: each set rebuilds the full array/struct chain at every level (O(children)
  per level) — a structural-sharing/persistent-Vec (e.g. a 32-way trie) would make array writes
  sub-linear; and intermediate alias chains make field/nested reads a hair slower than the old
  flat-cell layout. (was 5)

  The Model P semantics were locked by a 10-area audit (13 tests in src/tests/semantics.rs,
  caeef52): RefLocation holds only (root Cell + path), root replacement invalidates incompatible
  paths deterministically, reads/binds/args are value snapshots, aliases write through with
  replace-at-leaf at final positions, cycles still fail with "cyclic reference", and %v keeps
  the Ref(...) wrapper while %s/%x/%j/%q deref. The 32-way persistent vector remains a SEPARATE
  future optimization, deliberately not part of this change. Explicit trigger criteria: re-open
  the trie only when a REALISTIC write-heavy workload (nested struct transforms / large-array
  updates in actual scripts) shows `set` consuming more than ~30–40% of runtime; the
  microbenchmark alone (even set_elem_big, already 4.2× faster) is not sufficient justification.
  The Rc<Vec> model stays: simple, correct, well-tested (463 tests), and it already delivers the
  targeted win.

Bug
===

- Resolved: no tail calls and no recursion-depth guard — deep or infinite recursion used to
  overflow the native stack and abort (panic = "abort") with no diagnostic. The evaluator now
  bounds call depth: MAX_CALL_DEPTH (2048) nested calls; past that, `RecursionError: call depth
  limit (2048) exceeded …` points at the offending call (file:line:col + caret) and carries the
  live call chain (innermost 40 frames, then "… N more frame(s) omitted"). The interpreter runs
  on a thread with a large explicit stack (256 MB, main()) so the guard — not the native stack —
  is what bounds recursion; 2048 was calibrated against measured frame costs (~28–44 KB per call
  level in debug builds, ~6 KB in release; the tree-walker is stack-hungry). Recursion near the
  limit still works; `loop` remains the unbounded iteration form. Residual: `(eval …)`-driven
  self-nesting, deeply nested data (copy/render), and deep parser nesting have their own native
  recursion the call guard does not count. (was 6)

- Open: tail-call optimization — tail-recursive functions (and their calls inside if/match/
  block tails) would run in constant stack, making tail recursion unbounded and widening the
  usable recursion headroom far beyond the guard. The guard above means this is no longer a
  crash hazard; it is a performance/expressiveness item. (from the item above)

- Resolved: thread_local bookkeeping + LAST_ERROR_SPAN attribution. Measured (release,
  wall-clock median-of-5) before committing any fix: the per-node
  `LAST_ERROR_SPAN` write (eval entry, main.rs:1110) costs ~3% on the most node-dense
  workload (tight loop 1.00 s → 0.97 s with the write removed) and ~0 on native/scalar
  paths — and it is ~100% RefCell borrow-check overhead: `std::cell::Cell<Option<Span>>`
  (Span is Copy) recovers the whole delta (Cell 0.97 s ≈ no-write 0.97 s), so the per-node
  write stays (it is what makes Lisp-level errors point at the failing node inside a
  function body). Fragility fixed at the three raise sites that ran "under" a stale span
  (pointing at the last evaluated argument, not the failing call): arithmetic builtins
  (call() before `builtin`), native module functions (invoke() before `(f.call)(vals)`),
  and `$`/format_value (after arg evaluation). Probes before → after (first line:col):
  `(div 5 0)` 1:8 → 1:1; `(io.open "file:///nonexistent?mode=r")` (with use "io")
  1:21 → 1:12; `($ "%q")` 1:4 → 1:1. Descriptor (2226) / recursion-guard (2442) /
  (eval) 2169-2182 already set call_span. Three regression tests in
  src/tests/errors.rs; 473 tests green debug+release, fmt/clippy clean. CALL_TRACE /
  LAST_TRACE (push/pop per Lisp call + clone-on-error) and EVAL_SOURCES (per eval
  boundary) intentionally untouched — off the per-node path and cold; a full span-threaded
  redesign (span in Flow/Error, delete the global) remains a separate item if ever wanted.
  (was 8)

Code bugs found by the Drift.md audit, re-verified against `1c47835` and re-referenced to current
line numbers. Provenance is marked `(Drift.md Gn)`. The documentation half of the audit is closed:
`spec_en.md` is now the source of truth and supersedes `spec.txt` (archived) and `README.md` for
every A/B/C/D-class item, so those need no work here.

- Resolved: `$` errors no longer print a doubled `FormatError: FormatError:` prefix. The Display arm
  for `Error::Format` prepends `FormatError: `, and seven raise sites baked the same literal into the
  message they handed it: the two out-of-range messages in `emit_capture` and, in the format scanner,
  the missing-`%~` message and the capture-index-below-1 message, each written twice because the
  scanner has two selector branches. Dropped the literal at all seven; the Display arm is now the only
  place the string appears, so `grep '"FormatError: '` has exactly one hit. Drift.md G1 claimed twelve
  sites; seven was the verified count. The one test that asserted the doubled inner message
  (src/tests/strings.rs, `format_tilde_regex_selectors_require_pending_match`) was asserting the bug —
  this is Drift.md F4 — so it was corrected, and the missing guard F4 points at was added as
  `format_errors_never_repeat_the_category_prefix`: it walks 27 error-producing forms of `$` and
  asserts the *rendered* text contains `FormatError: ` exactly once. 480 tests green debug+release,
  fmt/clippy clean. spec_en.md §15.11, §18 and Appendix C updated. (Drift.md G1, F4)

- Resolved: `%s` on a nested string used JSON escapes the reader could not parse back, so `eval`
  silently corrupted data. `render_nested` routed `Value::Str` through `json_string`, which escapes
  every char ≤ 0x1F as `\u00XX`, while the reader's escape arm dropped the backslash, so `\u0001` read
  back as the five characters `u0001`. Now calls `lisp_string`, the reader's own escape set, with a
  comment recording why `json_string` is the wrong choice here. A/B on a fixture holding `a` 0x01 `b`:
  `(eval ($ "%s" [s]))` gave `Str("au0001b")` with `(eq … s)` false, now gives `Str("a\u{1}b")` with
  `(eq … s)` true. `%j` is deliberately untouched and still emits `\u0001`. Note the blast radius was
  narrow: a control character had to be inside a *nested* string, which in practice means one that
  arrived from `io.read` or from an HTTP body. Because the reader is now strict (G5 below),
  `(eval ($ "%j" s))` on such a string is a clean `ParseError` rather than a silently wrong value.
  Both statements are now historical: the `\u{HEX}` escape below lets source produce a control
  character directly, and a raw control byte in source is legal, so `%q`, `%x` and a nested `%s` all
  read back. Regression test
  `nested_percent_s_escapes_with_the_readers_own_escapes` writes the fixture from the test side, since
  no in-language expression can produce a control character. spec_en.md §15.2 and Appendix C updated.
  (Drift.md G2, D3)

- Resolved: the lexer dropped the backslash of an unknown escape, so `"\q"` was the string `q` and
  `"\0"` was the string `0`. Classified in the audit as a design choice rather than a bug, so the
  direction was put to the user, who chose a `ParseError` over keeping the backslash: a `"..."`
  string now accepts exactly the six documented escapes and `other => other` is gone. The message
  names the character, `ParseError: unknown escape sequence \q`. This makes the escape set total and
  matches how the language already treats an unknown specifier (`%z`), a trailing `%`, and a bad
  width. Nothing in src/tests or examples relied on the fallthrough. The six accepted escapes are
  unchanged (verified against a real byte dump: `"a\nb"` writes `a` LF `b`), and a `'...'` raw string
  still takes no escapes, so `'ab\qcd'` is still the six characters it always was. Two consequences
  worth knowing: a Windows path such as `"C:\temp"` was always going to be a tab, because `\t` is a
  real escape, so a raw string is still the way to write it; and a backslash at the very end of the
  input now reports `unterminated string`, as before. Regression test
  `unknown_string_escapes_are_a_parse_error`. spec_en.md §1.2, §15.2 and Appendix C updated. Note the
  new ParseError gets a stale column in the REPL, because the lexer still does not set
  `PARSE_ERROR_SPAN` — that was the B10 item below, now resolved. (Drift.md G5)

- Resolved: a deferred Ctrl-C in the REPL silently swallowed the next line typed. The `INTERRUPTED`
  check sat at the top of the session loop, before `console.read_line` blocks, so a SIGINT at an idle
  prompt printed nothing at all; the flag survived until the *next* line was evaluated, where
  `check_interrupted` turned it into `Error::Interrupted` and the `Error::Interrupted` arm cancelled
  that line — so the notice appeared only after the user typed their next line, and that line's result
  was never echoed. Verified over a pty before the fix: after SIGINT at an idle prompt the tty is
  silent; typing `(add 1 2)` yields `repl: interrupted (:c continues, :q quits)` and no `3`.
  The fix keeps the data loss out without making the read interruptible, because a blocked read cannot
  be woken by a signal here: `std` retries `EINTR` internally, so there is no way to observe the signal
  before the read returns. The session now checks for a pending interrupt again immediately after the
  read returns and before evaluating, reports the notice, and then evaluates the line in hand. That is
  the correct line to evaluate, because the tty driver flushes its input queue on Ctrl-C (VINTR), so
  whatever was being typed is already gone and the read returns the line typed *after* the cancel.
  Re-verified over a pty after the fix, all three positions: Ctrl-C at an idle prompt then `(add 1 2)`
  echoes `3`; Ctrl-C with `(add 1 ` typed, then `2)` Enter, evaluates the fragment `2)` and reports
  `unexpected token RParen`, which is what a real REPL does with a fragment typed after a cancel; and
  Ctrl-C during a 400M-iteration `loop` printed the notice and left the session usable. A Ctrl-C
  during evaluation used to cancel that line, on the `Error::Interrupted` arm; that arm is gone, see
  the Ctrl-C feature entry below, which supersedes that half of this fix.
  New per-thread test hook `REPL_INTERRUPT_AFTER_READ`, mirroring `REPL_INTERRUPT`, for the position
  the old hook cannot reach; both are saved and restored by `InterruptedGuard`. Regression tests
  `repl_interrupt_before_the_prompt_is_reported_and_redraws`,
  `repl_interrupt_during_the_read_keeps_the_line_that_follows_it` and
  `repl_interrupt_hooks_are_restored_when_the_session_ends`, each asserting the whole captured session
  transcript. spec_en.md §20.10 rewritten with a three-row table of where the keypress lands, and
  Appendix C items 9 and the deferred-notice bullet added. (Drift.md G3, A11, F2)

- Residual, by choice: the notice for a Ctrl-C that lands during a read is printed when that read
  returns, not at the moment of the keypress, so the user sees it after their next Enter. Making it
  immediate needs either a poll/select loop on the tty (a new dependency or hand-written FFI) or I/O
  from the `ctrlc` handler thread, which `ctrlc` does allow since it runs the closure off the signal
  context, but that needs a session-owned terminal handle plus coordination against the prompt. The
  user chose the data-loss fix only. spec_en.md §20.10 and Appendix C state the timing, so the behaviour
  is documented rather than implied.

- Open: `r+`/`w+` writes land at the reader's prefetch offset, not the logical stream position.
  src/modules/io.rs:266 does `stream.reader.get_mut().write_all(...)` with no `seek`, and the
  `BufReader` has already advanced the OS cursor to EOF. Re-verified: a 12-byte file, one `io.read`
  of `"aaa"`, then `io.write "ZZZ"` produces 15 bytes with `ZZZ` at offset 12, not 4.
  src/tests/io.rs:179-181 only passes because its fixture is a single short line with no meaningful
  prefetch. Fix options: (a) drop the `BufReader` for writable handles and use one `File` with an
  explicit `seek` per operation; (b) track a logical position beside the buffer and seek before each
  write. (a) is simpler and does not affect read-only buffer reuse. (Drift.md G4, A4, C1)

- Resolved: `pow` with a negative integer exponent reported `IntegerOverflow`, which is the wrong
  error. The integer path folded a failed `u32::try_from(-1)` and a genuine `checked_pow` overflow into
  the same `.ok_or_else(|| IntegerOverflow)`, so `(pow 2 -1)` claimed an overflow when the real result
  is 0.5. `(pow 2.0 -1)` returned 0.5 correctly, so the two paths disagreed about the answer to the
  same question. The user chose the float answer, which is what the language already did for the
  adjacent case: `(pow 2 1.5)` had always returned a float, so an integer base never implied integer
  arithmetic, and a negative exponent was the only exponent that errored. Only a non-negative exponent
  now takes the integer path; a negative one falls through to `powf`, so `(pow 2 -1)` is 0.5,
  `(pow 2 -2)` is 0.25, and `(pow 0 -1)` is `Inf` as `(pow 0.0 -1)` always was.
  A genuine overflow is untouched and is still `IntegerOverflow`: `(pow 2 63)` and `(pow 2 64)`
  overflow, and the boundary is exact — `(pow -2 63)` is `i64::MIN` and fits, `(pow -2 64)` overflows.
  The float path never overflows, it saturates, so `(pow 2.0 1000)` is a very large float.
  Three tests: `pow_uses_integer_arithmetic_only_for_a_non_negative_integer_exponent` asserts the type
  as well as the value, since the rule is about which arithmetic ran;
  `pow_still_reports_a_genuine_integer_overflow` guards the change against over-reaching, and passes
  both before and after by design; `pow_by_a_negative_exponent_of_zero_is_infinite` covers zero.
  spec_en.md §15 with a worked table, the builtin table row, and the Appendix C bullet removed.
  (Drift.md G7)

- Open: a missing bracket before a `[` selector is silently reinterpreted as a function call. The
  postfix parser requires strict adjacency — `token.span.start == e.span.end` (src/main.rs:600-603) —
  and gives no diagnostic when the guard fails; the bracketed form simply becomes the next argument. So
  `(p box [1])` reports `ArityError: function expects 1, got 2` instead of anything pointing at the
  space. This is the single most likely typo in the language and it produces a baffling message. Fix:
  detect the near-miss in the postfix loop and raise a parse error naming the position and suggesting
  the adjacent form. (Drift.md G7)

- Resolved: REPL lexer errors reported a column taken from a stale span, putting the caret past the end
  of the line. Only the parser set `PARSE_ERROR_SPAN`; the lexer never did, so for a lexer error
  `repl_diagnostic` read whatever the program's own parse left behind and then clamped it with
  `.min(line.len())`, which yields a column of `len + 1`. Re-verified: typing `"abc` in a session
  reported `<repl>:1:5` with the caret one past the end of a four-character line, while the same input
  from a file reported `1:1`. Root cause found while fixing it: the byte-offset table was built *after*
  the token loop, on the success path only, so a lexer error had no correct span available at all. `lex`
  is now a wrapper that builds the table up front, clears `PARSE_ERROR_SPAN` first, and stamps the start
  of the token being read on failure; the token loop itself moved to `lex_tokens` and reports its current
  character index through an out-parameter, since the loop counts characters and has no business knowing
  the diagnostics slice with byte offsets. Every lexer error now points at the start of its own token:
  `unterminated string`, `unknown escape sequence \q`, the three `\u{...}` errors, and `unclosed
  array` all report the offending token's column. Parser errors are byte-for-byte unchanged, and the
  `(eval "...")` path still reports at the `eval` call, because that arm deliberately clears the span.
  A side effect worth keeping: a lexer error after a non-ASCII prefix is now positioned correctly,
  where before it was clamped to column 1 — `"éé" "abc` reports column 6, the second quote. Regression
  tests `lexer_errors_point_at_their_own_token_and_never_at_a_stale_span` (five sources, each after a
  real span is left behind at a larger offset), `lexer_error_columns_count_characters_not_bytes`, and
  `repl_lexer_errors_are_not_reported_against_the_previous_line` (a full session transcript, with the
  error deliberately not in column 1 so a stale span and a cleared one differ). All three were confirmed
  to fail against the pre-fix code. spec_en.md §18 and §20.8 updated. (Drift.md B10)

Diagnostic inconsistencies — real, but low severity, and each is a defensible design choice rather than
a wrong result:

- Open: `expect` renders its operands with `debug_render` (src/main.rs:2136), so a failure reads
  `expected Int(2), got Int(1)`. Every other user-facing value rendering in the language goes through
  `%s`. The semantics are correct; only the rendering is out of step. (Drift.md G6, C5)
- Open: `break` and `continue` check arity in inverted order. `break` tests `l == 0` first
  (src/main.rs:2057) and only then `args.len() > 1` (2060); `continue` calls `need(args, 0, ...)` first
  (2070). Consequence: `(break 1 2)` outside a loop is a `BreakOutsideLoop`, so break's arity error is
  unreachable outside a loop, while `(continue 1)` is always an arity error. Neither ordering is wrong,
  but they should match. spec_en.md §12 now documents the asymmetry, so fixing the code means updating
  that section. (Drift.md A17)
- Open: the format-error taxonomy is split. A bad fixed width gives a named sub-message
  (`FormatTypeError: %7b supports widths 8, 16, 32, or 64`, src/main.rs:2752/2768) while an
  unrepresentable width gives a bare `invalid binary width` (2660) with no sub-message at all. One error
  class, two shapes. (Drift.md B5)

Dead code — wrong nowhere, but it was a parameter threaded through 9 signatures and never read:

- Resolved: `match_depth` was passed through `eval`/`call`/`apply_index`/`range_positions`/`values`/
  `define_let`/`eval_repl_line`/`run_repl`/`invoke`/`format_value`, incremented at the two `match`
  branches, and then never compared, printed, or branched on anywhere. The `loop_depth` half of the
  `eval`-inherits-depth rule is real; the `match_depth` half was vestigial. Provable by inspection —
  no comparison site existed. Removed from all 9 signatures and every call site, in `src/main.rs`,
  `src/tests/mod.rs` and `src/tests/repl.rs`. (Drift.md A14)
- The removal caught a real trap, which is why it is worth recording. The `match` arm read
  `eval(&p[0], env, l, m + 1)`: the `m + 1` was a naive `, l, m` rewrite turned into `l + 1`, which
  incremented `loop_depth` instead. `break_inside_match_inside_loop_exits_the_loop` failed, because a
  `break` inside a `match` started counting as inside a loop. The lesson is that the two parameters
  are interchangeable to a textual rewrite and not interchangeable in meaning; the test that
  distinguishes them is the one that already existed.
- A sweep for the same shape found nothing else. `call_span` and `execution_span` forward a lot too,
  but both terminate in real reads (`LAST_ERROR_SPAN.set`, `CALL_TRACE.push`, `repl_prompt`). There is
  no `#[allow(dead_code)]` anywhere, no TODO/FIXME/unimplemented marker, and no unused const, which
  rustc would flag.

Classified NOT code bugs — do not file these as fixes:

- A3 (`validate_module` rejects every non-descriptor member, so a module can never carry a data field)
  and A7 (`break` in a function called from a loop is `BreakOutsideLoop`, since only the literal `loop`
  form catches `Flow::Break`) are both stricter than the old spec but internally coherent. Both are now
  documented as they behave.
- The remaining G7 rows are documentation gaps, not defects: `(mod 7.0 2)` is a `TypeError` by design,
  `InvalidShiftCount` is a correct and well-named error, and `(bit-shl 1 63)` overflowing is correct.
  All three are now in spec_en.md §8 and §15.11. The fourth G7 row, `pow` with a negative exponent, was
  filed here too and turned out not to be a documentation gap at all — the user showed that the two
  `pow` paths disagreed about the same answer, which no amount of documenting makes coherent. It is
  resolved above.
- All of A, B, C, D and E is documentation drift, now resolved by spec_en.md.
- Re-confirmed as not-a-bug during the REPL pass, not changed: the echo of a function is `<fn>` and a
  native function is `<native fn>`, not the lisp form. spec.txt asked for the lisp form; spec_en.md §20.2
  documents `<fn>` in a table, so under the current source of truth there is no drift to fix. It does
  mean a function cannot be copied back out of a session, which is a real limitation and a reasonable
  thing to revisit, but it is a documented design choice rather than a defect.
- F1-F12 are coverage gaps rather than product bugs, but F1-F5 are *defective tests* — they assert
  something other than what their names claim, which is precisely why the bugs above survived. F4 is
  done, as part of the G1 fix above. The rest are worth fixing so the remaining fixes are actually
  guarded:
  - F1: Resolved. `repl_accepts_an_optional_label_and_eof_returns_null` passed a label and never
    inspected the prompt, and asserted the program value was 2 rather than `Null`. It now asserts
    `breakpoint@<test>:2> ` for a labelled session, `<test>:1> ` with no `@` for an unlabelled one, and
    `Null` from a bare `(repl)` on EOF, keeping the `:c` case as a separate assertion.
  - F2: Resolved with G3. The old test drove `REPL_INTERRUPT`, which feeds the same top-of-loop branch
    as the real bug, so it could not fail. It is now `repl_interrupt_before_the_prompt_is_reported_and_redraws`
    and asserts the full transcript, joined by the two after-read tests above. Note the limit recorded
    in the G3 residual: a faithful unit test of the swallow needs the process-wide `INTERRUPTED` atomic,
    which is unsafe to set in a 500-test parallel suite, so the after-read tests drive the per-thread
    hook and lock the notice placement and that the line runs. The real-flag path was verified over a
    pty instead. Putting a hook in `check_interrupted` was rejected: it ran on every function call and
    every loop iteration. It no longer does — it is only called around the blocking reads in `io.rs`
    now — but the rejected shape does not come back, because the evaluator checks an atomic directly.
  - F3: Resolved. The `/dev/tty` arm was asserted only negatively, so the stdout-vs-tty routing was
    untested. `ReplConsole` now exposes the decision as `ReplSink` (`Captured`, `ProgramStdout`,
    `ProgramStderr`, `Terminal`) through `echo_sink`/`notify_sink`, which the write methods dispatch on,
    so the routing table is a testable value rather than a shape buried in two `match`es. Three tests
    cover it, and the terminal arm is exercised for real by constructing a `Tty` console over a
    temporary file, since the routing does not care which file it is: the bytes are asserted to land
    there and nowhere else, and `repl_capture_hook_never_hijacks_a_real_session` locks that a collecting
    capture hook does not divert a terminal session into a buffer.
  - F4: Resolved with G1. The strings tests asserted inner sub-messages and never the rendered text,
    so the doubled prefix was structurally invisible. `format_errors_never_repeat_the_category_prefix`
    now asserts the rendered text for 27 error-producing forms of `$`.
  - F5: `FormatTypeError` has zero assertions by name, and the width paths have no test at all.

Feature
=======

- Resolved: Ctrl-C opens a session at the point it interrupted, instead of ending the run. This is
  the user's request, and it also removes a data-loss bug the old behaviour caused in `io.read`. The
  change is small: `check_interrupted` became `interrupt_into_repl`, the two evaluator check sites
  (`eval`, and the `loop` arm of `call`) now pass the scope, the loop depth and the current span, and
  the flag is consumed before the session installs its guard, so `:c` cannot come straight back into
  another session. `run_repl` was split into `run_repl_on`, which takes an already-open console and an
  optional preface, so the notice can be printed before the first prompt of a session nobody asked
  for. `:c` resumes the interrupted computation, `:q` ends the run with `Quit` as in any session, and
  Ctrl-C inside a session opens a nested one, so a program that will not finish is stopped by typing
  `:q` rather than by pressing Ctrl-C twice. Sessions nest exactly as `(repl)` nests.
- The session is opened with the scope that was live where the interrupt landed, which is what makes
  the feature worth having. The `loop` check hands over the loop's own scope, so the counter can be
  read and corrected: verified over a pty on a program searching for 19999999 by counting, where `i`
  read `860354` and `(set i 20000000)` then `:c` finished the search at once. The loop depth is passed
  too, so `(break)` typed in such a session still leaves the loop: verified over the same pty, where
  `(break 42)` produced `answer=42`. `:bt` resolves against the right source, so the frame is there.
  The prompt names the line the program was on, which is the enclosing `loop` or the innermost
  expression under evaluation, whichever check saw the signal first — on that program it reads line 4,
  7 or 9 from run to run, and each is a line the program was really on. That is the right answer: a
  signal carries no position, so there is nothing more precise to report.
- `Error::Interrupted` is now only the no-controlling-terminal case, so the `Error::Interrupted` arm in
  the session loop became dead and was removed. The evaluator can no longer produce that error; the
  generic per-line diagnostic path handles it correctly if a fallback ever reaches a session. The
  fallback exists because a session needs a terminal: with none, Ctrl-C still ends the run, with
  `Interrupted` and no mention of the missing terminal, since someone who pressed Ctrl-C to stop a
  program did not ask about the terminal. Verified by running a long program under
  `start_new_session=True`, which detaches the controlling terminal: exit 1, `Interrupted`, a caret
  and a call trace.
- A native function cannot open a session itself, because `NativeFunction` closures are
  `Fn(Vec<Value>)` and are not given the environment; threading one through would touch every native
  in every module. Instead `check_interrupted` records the interrupt in a thread-local and the
  evaluator opens the session at its next check, a node or a loop iteration away. This is the fix for
  the `io.read` data loss, which is the more common interactive case: previously a Ctrl-C at an
  `io.read` prompt ended the run *after* the read returned, so the line the user typed after the
  cancel was read and then thrown away. Verified both ways over a pty on a prompt-and-read program:
  before, `first> ^C`, `hello`, then `prog.lisp:2:34: Interrupted`; after, `hello` is used by the
  program and a session opens at the next line. The cost is that the session opens a node late rather
  than exactly at the read, and not at all if the program ends first, which is stated in spec_en.md
  §20.10 and Appendix C. The flag is thread-local because a native and the evaluator driving it are
  always the same thread, and that is also what makes the wiring testable without racing the
  process-wide `INTERRUPTED`.
- `source_for_span` was added because a span is not always in the context on top of the source stack:
  a program running inside a session line has the line on top, and an offset into the program can be
  past the end of it, so the prompt used to fall back to a bare `repl> `. The stack is now searched
  from the top for the first context long enough to hold the span. The `(repl)` form keeps using the
  top, because its span does live there.
- Five tests. `a_ctrl_c_opens_a_session_and_the_program_continues_after_it` drives the evaluator
  wiring end to end, through `eval` and `take_interrupt`, and asserts the notice lands before the
  first prompt and that the program still reaches its end value.
  `a_ctrl_c_session_can_quit_the_whole_run` locks `:q`. `a_ctrl_c_session_reads_and_writes_the_scope_it_was_opened_with`
  is the property that makes the loop case work, and drives `interrupt_into_repl` directly, because a
  pending interrupt set before a program starts is consumed by its first node, long before any loop
  exists — so the loop scope itself was verified over a pty instead.
  `a_ctrl_c_before_a_session_starts_leaves_no_interrupt_behind` locks the flag consumption that stops
  `:c` from looping on itself. `a_native_that_notices_a_ctrl_c_hands_it_to_the_evaluator` and
  `a_source_span_resolves_against_the_source_that_contains_it` cover the handoff and the source
  lookup. spec_en.md §18, §20.8, §20.9 and §20.10 rewritten, Appendix C item 10 added.

- Resolved: `\u{HEX}` escape in a `"..."` string, naming one Unicode scalar value. `HEX` is one or
  more hex digits in either case with no fixed width and no required leading zeros, so `\u{41}`,
  `\u{0041}` and `\u{000041}` are the same character. A value above the last code point 10FFFF is
  refused, and so is the surrogate range D800 to DFFF in either case, because no character has one of
  those values; a value too long for 32 bits lands in the first message too, since the parse cannot
  fail any other way once the digits are known to be hex. A malformed form — no brace, no closing
  brace, a non-hex digit, a space, a minus sign — is one `ParseError` reading `a unicode escape must
  be written \u{HEX}`, and the empty pair `\u{}` gets `a unicode escape needs at least one digit`.
  The braces are required: there is deliberately no brace-less form, so the JSON `\u0001` that `%j`
  writes stays unreadable, and `(eval ($ "%j" s))` on a string with a control character is a
  `ParseError` rather than a wrong value. That direction was put to the user, who chose the strict
  braced form over also accepting JSON's. Two old limits fall out of this. The reader can now build a
  control character from source, which it could not do at all before, so the G2 regression test no
  longer needs a file fixture to obtain one (it keeps the file, since io.read is how one really
  arrives). And because a raw control byte is legal in source, `%q`, `%x` and a nested `%s` all read
  back a string holding one, so `%j` is the only specifier that does not. New tests:
  `unicode_escapes_name_one_code_point`, `unicode_escapes_reject_non_scalar_values`,
  `control_characters_round_trip_through_every_specifier_but_json`. spec_en.md gains §1.6, with
  §1.2, §15.2 and §21.3 updated. (Drift.md D1)

- Resolved: `lisp_string` now emits `\u{HEX}` for every control character, so the specifiers that
  write a string as source produce valid, copyable Lisp rather than something that merely re-reads.
  Any control character — C0 (`0x00`-`0x1F`), DEL (`0x7F`) and C1 (`0x80`-`0x9F`), which is exactly
  Unicode `Cc` and so exactly Rust's `char::is_control` — is written as `\u{HEX}` with uppercase hex
  digits and no leading zeros: `0x01` is `\u{1}`, `0x0B` is `\u{B}`, `0x7F` is `\u{7F}`, `0x9B` is
  `\u{9B}`. Line feed, carriage return and tab keep their short escapes `\n`, `\r`, `\t`; they are
  control characters too, but they already had an escape, and their own form is the readable one.
  Nothing else is escaped, so `é`, an emoji, U+00A0, U+00AD and U+10FFFF all stay literal and a
  string of ordinary text comes out looking like itself. The direction was put to the user, who
  confirmed that exact table. This applies to `%q`, to a string nested in `%x`, and to a string
  nested in `%s`, because all three go through this one function. `%j` is deliberately untouched and
  stays JSON with its brace-less `\u0001`. Round-trip tests assert `(eq (eval ($ "%q" v)) v)` and the
  same for `%x` and a nested `%s` for all 65 control code points, not a sample, and a pinned-form
  test fixes the emitted text so a future change cannot slip back to a raw byte. New tests:
  `every_control_character_round_trips_through_q_x_and_nested_s`,
  `control_characters_are_rendered_as_unicode_escapes`, `printable_characters_are_rendered_literally`.
  486 tests green debug+release, fmt/clippy clean. spec_en.md §15.3 gains the full table and the
  rationale, with §1.6, §15.2 and the §15.2 summary row updated. Two things deliberately left:
  `%v` still uses Rust's own debug escaping, so its `\u{1}` is lowercase hex where the reader's own
  forms are uppercase — the reader takes both, so this is cosmetic; and `%j` still does not read
  back, which is correct for JSON.

- Resolved: `use` AST special-case removed — modules are defined and registered in two
  separate steps, and `use` evaluates its argument normally (no AST peek). `(let name {…})`
  defines a module (plain binding); `(use "name")` evaluates its argument to a name string,
  then either loads a native (`io`/`str`/`http`, binding like `let` — duplicate in the same
  scope stays DuplicateBindingError) or looks up the existing binding, validates the module
  syntax and registers it in the current scope (at most once per scope — ModuleError
  "module `X` is already registered"; shadowing in a child scope registers afresh, like
  `let`). Unknown names → NameError "module `X` is not defined"; the old
  `(use (let m {…}))` shape is gone (the arg evaluates to Null → TypeError "expected
  string"). This also kills the pre-existing ordering leak by construction: validation now
  always happens before anything is committed, so a failed registration binds/registers
  nothing (probes: `(use (let m 5))` left `m=5` bound; `(use (let m {f: 1}))` left `m`
  bound to the broken struct). Env gained a per-scope `modules` list; new Error::Module
  variant; src/tests/modules.rs converted to the two-step form + 4 new registration tests;
  core.rs reserved-name check updated; spec.txt §2.1 and README "### Module descriptors"
  updated. `examples/quick.lisp` still uses the old one-step shape (user WIP, untouched).
  (was 10)

- Resolved: spec.type per-argument alternatives + vocabulary validation. Each of the `arity` (fixed integer) entries in `type` is a single type name (exact match), a
  non-empty array of type names (per-argument alternatives: the argument independently matches
  any member; allowed combinations are the cross product — never whole-signature overloads), or
  the `"any"` wildcard — which is the top type and may appear ONLY as a standalone entry:
  `['int']` → exactly int, `[['int' 'float']]` → int|float, `['any']` → all value types,
  `[['int' 'any']]` → registration error ("spec.type `any` cannot be combined with alternative
  types"), never silently normalized. All names validated at registration against the vocabulary
  null|bool|int|float|string|array|struct|function|ref|any (unknown names and empty sets fail
  fast). Mismatch error widens to "expects one of X, Y, got Z" for sets; singleton messages
  unchanged. `return` stays declaration-only. Spec stays live: `set` into `spec.type` affects
  every subsequent call. 470 tests green (debug+release), fmt/clippy clean; README + spec.txt §2
  document the syntax and the alternatives-vs-overloads distinction. (was the polymorphic-argument hole)

- Target TDC 16.1 CPU (Or explore LLVM)
